\eqn{k' = \mathrm{ThreeFry}_k(c)}, hashing \eqn{k'} with the counters
\eqn{0, 1, 2, \ldots}, and advances the random state to \eqn{(k, c + 1)},
however many values it drew. Deriving keys from a key with a keyed hash is
the idea of the splittable generators of
Claessen & Pałka (2013); the keys and bits are exactly those
of Bradbury et al. (2018): \eqn{k'} is JAX's \code{fold_in(k, c)}, the key
of the \eqn{c}-th draw of JAX's \code{stateful_rng()}, whose counter has 32 bits
where this one has 64, and the draw is JAX's \verb{random.bits(k', shape)}. So the size of a draw does not change what later
